{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QanKug9LzuhtkKxFzUFUwR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full paper and the review context. Let me organize what I've read:\n\n**Paper**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n- 7 pages, anonymous submission\n- Compares three formulations for execution-free code verification: CLS (classification), CWM (code world models), TRAJ (trajectory-level scoring)\n- Two model families: Qwen3-4B and DeepSeek-R1-8B\n- Two complexity levels: function-level and repository-level\n- Key finding: No single formulation dominates; at function-level they're equivalent; at repo-level, CWM viability depends on tokenizer\n\n\n- Also introduces the SWE-bench-PerTest dataset\n\nThe review context shows several experimental runs they're planning or have completed: different configurations of DeepSeek models with CLS and CWM formulations at repo-level, construction of the SWE-bench-PerTest dataset with 467 instances and nearly 90,000 pairs, and a diagnostic re-repair proof of concept that appears to show promise.\n\nNow I'm setting up the formal review process by dispatching two parallel reviewers—one focusing on methodology and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then running through the full roundtable discussion with independent reviews, cross-sharing, convergence, and a consolidated final report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15874,"cache_read_input_tokens":25237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15874},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"1a509b14-bf4f-46ef-ad73-d6b6ae7d0721"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QanKug9LzuhtkKxFzUFUwR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've read the full 7-page paper and the review context. Now dispatching two parallel reviewers for the full roundtable review.\n\n- **R1**: Method + Experiments focus\n- **R2**: Novelty + Related Work focus"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15874,"cache_read_input_tokens":25237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15874},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"6a84720b-57fd-481c-9f70-21d393720cd9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QanKug9LzuhtkKxFzUFUwR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic paper review. Your focus areas are **Method** and **Experiments**. Evaluate using a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n\n**Core claim**: No single formulation for execution-free code verification dominates. Three formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = execution output prediction, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) are compared under controlled conditions across two model families (Qwen3-4B, DeepSeek-R1-8B) at two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level).\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. At function-level, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). CLS offers 6–9× inference speedup.\n2. At repository-level, formulation effectiveness becomes model-contingent: Qwen3 CWM suffers 93% unparseable outputs; DeepSeek CWM achieves only 11.8% unparseable and surpasses CLS by 2.1pp.\n3. CWM failure is tokenizer-specific, not formulation-inherent (cross-model reversal evidence).\n4. CLS and CWM show complementary error patterns: oracle ensemble +2.8pp on DeepSeek repo-level.\n5. Re-repair PoC: +4pp (not statistically significant, p=0.18), presented as future direction.\n\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- Problem: Given patch p and tests {t1,...,tk}, predict correctness without execution.\n- CLS: input=[code;ti], output∈{pass,fail}. Single token output. Robust to generation errors. \n- CWM: input=[code;ti], output=expected execution output (structured text). Verdict by parsing and comparing to reference. Challenge: format compliance.\n- TRAJ: input=[code;t1,...,tk], output∈{resolve,not_resolve}. Holistic, no per-test granularity. Cost independent of test count.\n- Training: LoRA (rank 64, α=128), lr=1e-4, batch size 16, 3 epochs, max seq len 8192. Two random seeds per config.\n\n**Experiments (Section 4)**:\n- **Dataset**: SWE-bench-PerTest — Docker re-evaluation of 500 SWE-bench Verified instances × 16 candidates. 467 successful (93.4%), yielding 89,664 per-test labels. 73/27 pass/fail ratio. Majority baseline 73.0%.\n- **Table 2 (func-level)**: Qwen CLS 88.4, CWM 87.2, TRAJ 86.3; DeepSeek CLS 86.9, CWM 86.8, TRAJ 84.2. Majority baseline 52.0.\n- **Table 3 (repo-level)**: DeepSeek CLS 81.45±0.92, CWM 84.2, TRAJ 75.4; Qwen CLS 82.4, CWM ~7.0 (93% unparseable).\n- **Table 4**: CWM unparseable rates — func-level 0%/0%; repo-level 93.0%/11.8%.\n- **Table 5**: Oracle ensemble CLS∪CWM = 87.0% (+2.8pp over best individual 84.2%).\n- **Table 6**: Re-repair PoC — control 34%, predicted 38% (+4pp, p=0.18), oracle 42% (+8pp).\n\n**Experiment design reference** (from review_context.yaml — the planned experiments; paper may not cover all):\n- DeepSeek CLS Repo-Level (two seeds: 82.1% and 80.8%, mean 81.45±0.92)\n- DeepSeek CWM Repo-Level Epoch-2 Eval (84.2% overall, 11.8% unparseable, 88.7% parseable accuracy)\n- SWE-bench-PerTest construction (467 instances, 89,664 pairs)\n- Diagnostic Re-Repair PoC (200 instances, +4pp not significant)\n\n**References** (11 total): Chen et al. 2023 (CodeT), Gu et al. 2024 (CRUXEval), Le et al. 2022 (CodeRL), Li et al. 2022 (AlphaCode), Lightman et al. 2024, Ni et al. 2023 (LEVER), Shinn et al. 2023 (Reflexion), Uesato et al. 2022, Zhang et al. 2018 (predictive mutation testing), Yang et al. 2024 (SWE-bench).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Method Evaluation**:\n   - Is the problem well-formulated?\n   - Are the three formulations clearly defined and fairly compared?\n   - Is the experimental design (controlled comparison) sound?\n   - Are the training setup choices reasonable and well-justified?\n   - Any methodological concerns (confounds, missing controls)?\n\n2. **Experiments Evaluation**:\n   - Is the dataset construction rigorous?\n   - Are the results convincing? Do they support the claims?\n   - Statistical rigor (TOST test, multi-seed, significance testing)\n   - Missing experiments or analyses\n   - Table/figure quality and information density\n\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with actionable suggestions)\n5. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions you'd want answered)\n6. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and real limitations. Do not inflate or deflate — calibrate to top ML venue acceptance bar (~25% acceptance rate).","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15874,"cache_read_input_tokens":25237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15874},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"85a9f726-585a-4c0d-a338-b0ad7854c45d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a39abe0cdfbebf679","tool_use_id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic paper review. Your focus areas are **Method** and **Experiments**. Evaluate using a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n\n**Core claim**: No single formulation for execution-free code verification dominates. Three formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = execution output prediction, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) are compared under controlled conditions across two model families (Qwen3-4B, DeepSeek-R1-8B) at two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level).\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. At function-level, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). CLS offers 6–9× inference speedup.\n2. At repository-level, formulation effectiveness becomes model-contingent: Qwen3 CWM suffers 93% unparseable outputs; DeepSeek CWM achieves only 11.8% unparseable and surpasses CLS by 2.1pp.\n3. CWM failure is tokenizer-specific, not formulation-inherent (cross-model reversal evidence).\n4. CLS and CWM show complementary error patterns: oracle ensemble +2.8pp on DeepSeek repo-level.\n5. Re-repair PoC: +4pp (not statistically significant, p=0.18), presented as future direction.\n\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- Problem: Given patch p and tests {t1,...,tk}, predict correctness without execution.\n- CLS: input=[code;ti], output∈{pass,fail}. Single token output. Robust to generation errors. \n- CWM: input=[code;ti], output=expected execution output (structured text). Verdict by parsing and comparing to reference. Challenge: format compliance.\n- TRAJ: input=[code;t1,...,tk], output∈{resolve,not_resolve}. Holistic, no per-test granularity. Cost independent of test count.\n- Training: LoRA (rank 64, α=128), lr=1e-4, batch size 16, 3 epochs, max seq len 8192. Two random seeds per config.\n\n**Experiments (Section 4)**:\n- **Dataset**: SWE-bench-PerTest — Docker re-evaluation of 500 SWE-bench Verified instances × 16 candidates. 467 successful (93.4%), yielding 89,664 per-test labels. 73/27 pass/fail ratio. Majority baseline 73.0%.\n- **Table 2 (func-level)**: Qwen CLS 88.4, CWM 87.2, TRAJ 86.3; DeepSeek CLS 86.9, CWM 86.8, TRAJ 84.2. Majority baseline 52.0.\n- **Table 3 (repo-level)**: DeepSeek CLS 81.45±0.92, CWM 84.2, TRAJ 75.4; Qwen CLS 82.4, CWM ~7.0 (93% unparseable).\n- **Table 4**: CWM unparseable rates — func-level 0%/0%; repo-level 93.0%/11.8%.\n- **Table 5**: Oracle ensemble CLS∪CWM = 87.0% (+2.8pp over best individual 84.2%).\n- **Table 6**: Re-repair PoC — control 34%, predicted 38% (+4pp, p=0.18), oracle 42% (+8pp).\n\n**Experiment design reference** (from review_context.yaml — the planned experiments; paper may not cover all):\n- DeepSeek CLS Repo-Level (two seeds: 82.1% and 80.8%, mean 81.45±0.92)\n- DeepSeek CWM Repo-Level Epoch-2 Eval (84.2% overall, 11.8% unparseable, 88.7% parseable accuracy)\n- SWE-bench-PerTest construction (467 instances, 89,664 pairs)\n- Diagnostic Re-Repair PoC (200 instances, +4pp not significant)\n\n**References** (11 total): Chen et al. 2023 (CodeT), Gu et al. 2024 (CRUXEval), Le et al. 2022 (CodeRL), Li et al. 2022 (AlphaCode), Lightman et al. 2024, Ni et al. 2023 (LEVER), Shinn et al. 2023 (Reflexion), Uesato et al. 2022, Zhang et al. 2018 (predictive mutation testing), Yang et al. 2024 (SWE-bench).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Method Evaluation**:\n   - Is the problem well-formulated?\n   - Are the three formulations clearly defined and fairly compared?\n   - Is the experimental design (controlled comparison) sound?\n   - Are the training setup choices reasonable and well-justified?\n   - Any methodological concerns (confounds, missing controls)?\n\n2. **Experiments Evaluation**:\n   - Is the dataset construction rigorous?\n   - Are the results convincing? Do they support the claims?\n   - Statistical rigor (TOST test, multi-seed, significance testing)\n   - Missing experiments or analyses\n   - Table/figure quality and information density\n\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with actionable suggestions)\n5. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions you'd want answered)\n6. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and real limitations. Do not inflate or deflate — calibrate to top ML venue acceptance bar (~25% acceptance rate).","uuid":"5c21a503-1448-4f8d-a7f6-ff8aba4055ce","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic paper review. Your focus areas are **Method** and **Experiments**. Evaluate using a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n\n**Core claim**: No single formulation for execution-free code verification dominates. Three formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = execution output prediction, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) are compared under controlled conditions across two model families (Qwen3-4B, DeepSeek-R1-8B) at two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level).\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. At function-level, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). CLS offers 6–9× inference speedup.\n2. At repository-level, formulation effectiveness becomes model-contingent: Qwen3 CWM suffers 93% unparseable outputs; DeepSeek CWM achieves only 11.8% unparseable and surpasses CLS by 2.1pp.\n3. CWM failure is tokenizer-specific, not formulation-inherent (cross-model reversal evidence).\n4. CLS and CWM show complementary error patterns: oracle ensemble +2.8pp on DeepSeek repo-level.\n5. Re-repair PoC: +4pp (not statistically significant, p=0.18), presented as future direction.\n\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- Problem: Given patch p and tests {t1,...,tk}, predict correctness without execution.\n- CLS: input=[code;ti], output∈{pass,fail}. Single token output. Robust to generation errors. \n- CWM: input=[code;ti], output=expected execution output (structured text). Verdict by parsing and comparing to reference. Challenge: format compliance.\n- TRAJ: input=[code;t1,...,tk], output∈{resolve,not_resolve}. Holistic, no per-test granularity. Cost independent of test count.\n- Training: LoRA (rank 64, α=128), lr=1e-4, batch size 16, 3 epochs, max seq len 8192. Two random seeds per config.\n\n**Experiments (Section 4)**:\n- **Dataset**: SWE-bench-PerTest — Docker re-evaluation of 500 SWE-bench Verified instances × 16 candidates. 467 successful (93.4%), yielding 89,664 per-test labels. 73/27 pass/fail ratio. Majority baseline 73.0%.\n- **Table 2 (func-level)**: Qwen CLS 88.4, CWM 87.2, TRAJ 86.3; DeepSeek CLS 86.9, CWM 86.8, TRAJ 84.2. Majority baseline 52.0.\n- **Table 3 (repo-level)**: DeepSeek CLS 81.45±0.92, CWM 84.2, TRAJ 75.4; Qwen CLS 82.4, CWM ~7.0 (93% unparseable).\n- **Table 4**: CWM unparseable rates — func-level 0%/0%; repo-level 93.0%/11.8%.\n- **Table 5**: Oracle ensemble CLS∪CWM = 87.0% (+2.8pp over best individual 84.2%).\n- **Table 6**: Re-repair PoC — control 34%, predicted 38% (+4pp, p=0.18), oracle 42% (+8pp).\n\n**Experiment design reference** (from review_context.yaml — the planned experiments; paper may not cover all):\n- DeepSeek CLS Repo-Level (two seeds: 82.1% and 80.8%, mean 81.45±0.92)\n- DeepSeek CWM Repo-Level Epoch-2 Eval (84.2% overall, 11.8% unparseable, 88.7% parseable accuracy)\n- SWE-bench-PerTest construction (467 instances, 89,664 pairs)\n- Diagnostic Re-Repair PoC (200 instances, +4pp not significant)\n\n**References** (11 total): Chen et al. 2023 (CodeT), Gu et al. 2024 (CRUXEval), Le et al. 2022 (CodeRL), Li et al. 2022 (AlphaCode), Lightman et al. 2024, Ni et al. 2023 (LEVER), Shinn et al. 2023 (Reflexion), Uesato et al. 2022, Zhang et al. 2018 (predictive mutation testing), Yang et al. 2024 (SWE-bench).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Method Evaluation**:\n   - Is the problem well-formulated?\n   - Are the three formulations clearly defined and fairly compared?\n   - Is the experimental design (controlled comparison) sound?\n   - Are the training setup choices reasonable and well-justified?\n   - Any methodological concerns (confounds, missing controls)?\n\n2. **Experiments Evaluation**:\n   - Is the dataset construction rigorous?\n   - Are the results convincing? Do they support the claims?\n   - Statistical rigor (TOST test, multi-seed, significance testing)\n   - Missing experiments or analyses\n   - Table/figure quality and information density\n\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with actionable suggestions)\n5. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions you'd want answered)\n6. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and real limitations. Do not inflate or deflate — calibrate to top ML venue acceptance bar (~25% acceptance rate)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"be2e4f80-9686-4e53-82b3-199f5061cc9a","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:55:10.402Z"}
