{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PfMs1f56hkpPiKgDk8aPEs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tWe presented the first controlled comparison of three formulations for execution-free code repair verification: per-test binary classification (\\cls{}), execution output generation (\\cwm{}), and trajectory-level scoring. Our central finding is that no single formulation dominates across all conditions; the design space is governed by a formulation $\\times$ model $\\times$ complexity interaction.\n5\t\n6\tAt function level, \\cls{} and \\cwm{} achieve near-equivalent accuracy (gap 1.24\\pp{} at 4B, TOST equivalence $p{=}0.007$); formulation choice matters less than model capacity and training data at this complexity tier. At repository level, formulation divergence amplifies dramatically (4B: 25.59\\pp{}; 8B: 19.31\\pp{}). Cross-model validation with DeepSeek-Coder confirms this divergence is formulation-inherent: both model families show \\cwm{} $\\gg$ \\cls{} degradation from function to repo level. The magnitude, however, is model-contingent (Qwen3: ${\\sim}$5/22\\pp{}; DeepSeek: ${\\sim}$12/25\\pp{} for \\cls{}/\\cwm{}). %%SCENARIO-A%% \\cwm{} additionally exhibits higher seed sensitivity (${\\sim}$5\\pp{} across seeds vs.\\ \\cls{}'s ${\\sim}$2\\pp{}), though epoch 2 and epoch 3 achieve comparable accuracy and show no overtraining under our protocol. Evaluation protocol itself introduces ${\\sim}$9\\pp{} accuracy variation for the same \\cwm{} checkpoint, a side-effect of the unparseable rate $\\to$ majority-class default chain that practitioners must control for. %%END-SCENARIO-A%%\n7\t\n8\tTwo formulation-level properties generalize independent of model choice. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity at equivalent trajectory-level accuracy: it identifies which specific tests a patch fails. This advantage concentrates in instances with $\\geq$2 fail-to-pass tests (31\\% of instances, which contribute 55\\% of total oracle advantage). Second, the 1-token classification formulation yields a 217$\\times$ throughput advantage over variable-length generation, an inherent property of the output-length asymmetry.\n9\t\n10\tError complementarity between formulations (oracle ensemble: 90.63\\%) suggests that the practitioner's choice need not be exclusive. Classification and generation exhibit complementary failure patterns. This motivates routing strategies that select formulations based on predicted instance complexity.\n11\t\n12\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n13\tRepo-level cross-model \\cwm{} results are preliminary (DeepSeek-Coder \\cwm{} training in progress); \\cls{} results are final. The repo-level same-size comparisons each use a single seed (4B-4B and 8B-8B); multi-seed DeepSeek-Coder \\cls{} evaluation is in progress. For \\cwm{}, validation loss diverges to NaN (DeepSeek-Coder), preventing standard early stopping and requiring checkpoint selection by validation accuracy. No downstream re-repair experiment validates whether per-test diagnostic signals translate to actual fix improvements. Pass-to-pass regression rates are negligible (1.04\\% mean, 0\\% median), meaning the per-test advantage currently concentrates in fail-to-pass discrimination; regression detection remains untested in practice. Training data comprises synthetic mutations generated by operator-level transformations (operator swap, off-by-one, return value, boundary removal). While prior work has established that synthetic mutants correlate with real fault detection~\\cite{andrews2005mutation,just2014mutants}, the coupling is imperfect: real bugs often involve multi-statement changes, semantic misunderstandings, or integration errors that no single mutation operator captures. Our repo-level evaluation partially mitigates this concern by using SWE-bench Verified test cases derived from real pull requests, but the underlying code changes remain synthetic. Transfer to naturally occurring developer bugs is untested. The oracle analysis assumes ground-truth test labels; real-world per-test advantage is upper-bounded by classifier accuracy.\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{Future work.}\n16\tCross-model repo-level validation (\\cls{} final, \\cwm{} preliminary) confirms the formulation-inherent divergence pattern; multi-seed replication with final \\cwm{} training runs would strengthen the statistical claims at this complexity tier. Error complementarity motivates complexity-aware routing that selects formulations per-instance. A pilot study with 8B-scale repair agents found no measurable benefit from per-test diagnostics over binary accept/reject feedback; translating diagnostic granularity into repair improvements requires further investigation. Integration with test-time scaling systems (best-of-$N$ with per-test reranking) is a natural application of the diagnostic granularity finding, though the repair-side value remains to be demonstrated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"d7418396-935e-4e3c-96b1-78d70c99771e","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:55:20.682Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TbsEwHCRPhy9UxiwQBLPfY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018in8Sbm5gAJQYpKCqgGTMJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/appendix.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13859,"cache_read_input_tokens":17872,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13859,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":39,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"dcb5a107-b617-4449-8f19-5826bf469aaf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018in8Sbm5gAJQYpKCqgGTMJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\appendix\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Oracle Accuracy-Utility Analysis}\n4\t\\label{app:oracle}\n5\t\n6\tWe stratify SWE-bench Verified instances by F2P test count $T$, comparing per-test vs.\\ trajectory-level oracle resolve rates under i.i.d.\\ and block-correlated error models (ICC $= 0.191$, from function-level TOST analysis; \\S\\ref{sec:equivalence}).\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{table}[h]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\small\n11\t\\caption{Oracle accuracy-utility frontier: per-test advantage stratified by F2P test count ($T$) on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances). Block-correlated model uses function-level ICC $= 0.191$ (from TOST equivalence analysis). P2P regression rate: 1.04\\% mean, 0\\% median.}\n12\t\\label{tab:oracle}\n13\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n14\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n15\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n16\t\\toprule\n17\t\\textbf{$T$} & \\textbf{Inst.\\%} & \\textbf{i.i.d.\\ (\\pp{})} & \\textbf{Block (\\pp{})} & \\textbf{Contrib.\\%} \\\\\n18\t\\midrule\n19\t$1$ & 68.7 & $-$0.04 & $-$0.09 & $\\approx$0 \\\\\n20\t$2$ & 19.1 & 18.49 & 31.69 & 55.3 \\\\\n21\t$3$--$5$ & 7.1 & 25.00 & 40.74 & $\\approx$26 \\\\\n22\t$\\geq 6$ & 5.1 & 30.00 & 40.06 & $\\approx$19 \\\\\n23\t\\midrule\n24\t\\textbf{All} & 100.0 & --- & \\textbf{10.93} & 100.0 \\\\\n25\t\\bottomrule\n26\t\\end{tabular}}\n27\t\\end{table}\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n30\t\\centering\n31\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_oracle_stratified.pdf}\n32\t\\caption{Oracle per-test advantage by F2P test count $T$. Advantage is zero at $T{=}1$ (68.7\\%) and concentrates at $T{=}2$ (19.1\\%, contributing 55.3\\% of total advantage).}\n33\t\\label{fig:oracle_stratified}\n34\t\\end{figure}\n35\t\n36\tTable~\\ref{tab:oracle} and Figure~\\ref{fig:oracle_stratified} reveal a sharp concentration: at $T = 1$ (68.7\\% of instances), per-test and trajectory-level are informationally equivalent. The advantage materializes at $T = 2$ (19.1\\%), where block-correlated advantage reaches 31.69\\pp{} (55.3\\% of total). The overall block-correlated advantage is 10.93\\pp{}, driven by the $T \\geq 2$ minority. Block correlation amplifies relative to i.i.d.: at $T = 2$, 31.69\\pp{} vs.\\ 18.49\\pp{} (1.7$\\times$). P2P regression is negligible (1.04\\% mean, 0\\% median); the practical value concentrates in F2P discrimination.\n37\t\n38\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity to ICC estimate.}\n39\tThe analysis above uses ICC $= 0.191$ from function-level TOST equivalence testing (\\S\\ref{sec:equivalence}), where mean cluster size $\\bar{k} \\approx 16.5$ yields DEFF $= 3.97$. At repository level, within-problem correlation is substantially stronger: ICC $= 0.3146$ with DEFF $= 114.4$ (\\S\\ref{sec:repo}), a consequence of the larger and more heterogeneous test suites in SWE-bench Verified problems. Under higher ICC, within-problem errors cluster more tightly and amplify the block-correlated per-test advantage at each $T$ stratum; the correction from i.i.d.\\ to block-correlated grows monotonically with ICC. The function-level ICC $= 0.191$ used in Table~\\ref{tab:oracle} thus provides a conservative lower bound on the per-test advantage. The repo-level correlation structure would yield an even larger gap between per-test and trajectory-level oracle resolve rates, which strengthens the case for per-test granularity at repository-level complexity.\n40\t\n41\t\\section{Ablation Studies}\n42\t\\label{app:ablations}\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{LoRA rank sensitivity.}\n45\tAll \\cwm{} experiments use LoRA rank $r{=}16$. To test whether this capacity constraint handicaps \\cwm{}, we train rank-32 variants (seed 42), doubling adapter parameters.\n46\t\n47\t\\begin{table}[h]\n48\t\\centering\n49\t\\small\n50\t\\caption{LoRA rank ablation for \\cwm{} 4B (seed 42, 3 epochs, lr $= 2{\\times}10^{-4}$). Rank-16 seed variance: 0.53\\pp{} (seeds 42 vs.\\ 456).}\n51\t\\label{tab:rank_ablation}\n52\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n53\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n54\t\\toprule\n55\t\\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{$\\alpha/r$} & \\textbf{Acc (\\%)} & \\textbf{Fail \\fone{}} & \\textbf{Pass \\fone{}} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n56\t\\midrule\n57\t$r{=}16$, $\\alpha{=}32$ & 2 & \\textbf{87.51} & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n58\t$r{=}32$, $\\alpha{=}32$ & 1 & 87.00 & 87.1 & 86.9 & $-$0.51 \\\\\n59\t$r{=}32$, $\\alpha{=}64$ & 2 & 87.04 & 87.27 & 86.79 & $-$0.47 \\\\\n60\t\\bottomrule\n61\t\\end{tabular}\n62\t\\end{table}\n63\t\n64\tBoth rank-32 variants fall within the 2-seed variance of the rank-16 baseline (0.53\\pp{} between seeds 42 and 456). The $\\alpha{=}64$ variant (matching the baseline $\\alpha/r{=}2$ ratio) rules out the confound that the $\\alpha{=}32$ result might reflect reduced effective scaling rather than rank insensitivity. This is consistent with \\cwm{}'s function-level performance being bounded by output-format constraints rather than adapter capacity.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Majority voting.}\n67\tMajority voting ($K{=}3$) decreases accuracy from 87.51\\% to 86.83\\% ($-$0.68\\pp{}): errors are systematic rather than stochastic, so sampling-based ensembles do not help.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Sequence-length ablation details.}\n70\tBoth formulations are trained with \\texttt{max\\_seq\\_length}$=$2,048. At inference, \\cwm{} reserves \\texttt{max\\_new\\_tokens}$=$1,024, leaving ${\\sim}$1,024 effective input tokens; \\cls{} allocates nearly all context to input (5,120--8,192 tokens). A matched-context ablation (\\cls{} 8B at 2,048 tokens) achieves 83.04\\%, only 1.44\\pp{} below the 8,192-token evaluation (84.48\\%), though truncation introduces 136 unparseable outputs (1.39\\% of test samples). The matched-context gap (\\cls{}@2,048 vs.\\ \\cwm{}@2,048) is 25.00\\pp{}---98\\% of the full 25.59\\pp{} gap---confirming that input context disparity explains at most 3\\% of the difference.\n71\t\n72\t\\section{Repo-Level Per-Mutation Breakdown}\n73\t\\label{app:repo_mutation}\n74\t\n75\tTable~\\ref{tab:mutation_breakdown} compares per-mutation accuracy at repo level. \\cls{} produces 0\\% unparseable outputs; all values are direct classification accuracy. \\cwm{} overall accuracy includes default-pass assignment for unparseable outputs (see \\S\\ref{sec:repo}).\n76\t\n77\t\\begin{table}[h]\n78\t\\centering\n79\t\\small\n80\t\\caption{Per-mutation test accuracy (\\%) at repo level (SWE-bench Verified, $n{=}9{,}784$). \\cls{} 8B reports two seeds; $\\Delta$ is mean 4B$\\to$8B gain for \\cls{}. \\cwm{} 4B per-mutation overall accuracy unavailable except gold (computed from parse rate 43.5\\%, parseable acc 23.5\\%).}\n81\t\\label{tab:mutation_breakdown}\n82\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n83\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n84\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n85\t\\toprule\n86\t\\textbf{Mutation} & \\textbf{$n$} & \\textbf{CLS 4B} & \\textbf{CLS 8B s42} & \\textbf{CLS 8B s123} & \\textbf{CWM 4B} & \\textbf{CWM 8B} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ CLS} \\\\\n87\t\\midrule\n88\tgold & 4,633 & 98.25 & 96.24 & 99.60 & 66.72 & 80.06 & $-$0.33 \\\\\n89\toperator\\_swap & 3,357 & 78.91 & 81.38 & 82.60 & --- & 54.57 & +3.08 \\\\\n90\toff\\_by\\_one & 183 & 58.47 & 86.89 & 79.20 & --- & 63.39 & \\textbf{+24.58} \\\\\n91\treturn\\_value & 562 & 67.97 & 75.44 & 66.40 & --- & 49.11 & +2.95 \\\\\n92\tboundary\\_removal & 1,049 & 46.90 & 46.90 & 44.90 & --- & 42.23 & $-$1.00 \\\\\n93\t\\bottomrule\n94\t\\end{tabular}}\n95\t\\end{table}\n96\t\n97\tTable~\\ref{tab:cwm_mutation_parse} shows that \\cwm{} generation quality degrades from 4B to 8B across all mutation types.\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{table}[h]\n100\t\\centering\n101\t\\small\n102\t\\caption{\\cwm{} parse rates and parseable-only accuracy per mutation type (seed 42). Parse rate decreases uniformly from 4B to 8B; parseable-only accuracy is below random binary chance (50\\%) for gold at both scales.}\n103\t\\label{tab:cwm_mutation_parse}\n104\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n105\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n106\t\\toprule\n107\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Parse Rate (\\%)}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{Parse Acc (\\%)}} \\\\\n108\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n109\t\\textbf{Mutation} & \\textbf{4B} & \\textbf{8B} & \\textbf{4B} & \\textbf{8B} \\\\\n110\t\\midrule\n111\tgold & 43.5 & 23.9 & 23.5 & 16.5 \\\\\n112\toperator\\_swap & 56.9 & 21.2 & 63.1 & 58.8 \\\\\n113\toff\\_by\\_one & 4.4 & 27.3 & 0.0 & 38.0 \\\\\n114\treturn\\_value & 48.8 & 26.9 & 27.0 & 56.9 \\\\\n115\tboundary\\_removal & 30.6 & 51.8 & 59.2 & 51.8 \\\\\n116\t\\bottomrule\n117\t\\end{tabular}\n118\t\\end{table}\n119\t\n120\tKey findings: (1)~Gold parse rate collapses from 43.5\\% to 23.9\\% at 8B---the simplest generation target (unmodified code, where all tests pass) degrades most, consistent with a fundamental generation bottleneck rather than task difficulty. (2)~Among parseable outputs, gold accuracy is 16.5--23.5\\%: the model generates outputs that fail exact-match even for correct code. (3)~\\texttt{off\\_by\\_one} is an anomaly: parse rate \\emph{increases} from 4.4\\% to 27.3\\%, but this is from an extremely low base ($n{=}8$ parseable at 4B), making both directions unreliable for this category.\n121\t\n122\t\\section{Training Hyperparameters}\n123\t\\label{app:hyperparams}\n124\t\n125\tTable~\\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists all hyperparameters used for fine-tuning. Both \\cls{} and \\cwm{} share the same LoRA configuration, optimizer, and training schedule; the only differences are per-device batch size, gradient accumulation steps (adjusted to maintain identical effective batch size of 32 across model sizes), and learning rate (DeepSeek-Coder uses $2{\\times}10^{-4}$ vs.\\ $5{\\times}10^{-5}$ for Qwen3, tuned on a held-out validation split).\n126\t\n127\t\\begin{table}[h]\n128\t\\centering\n129\t\\small\n130\t\\caption{Training hyperparameters for all configurations. Effective batch size is $\\text{per-device} \\times \\text{grad.\\ accum.} = 32$ in all cases. \\traj{} models use lr $= 2{\\times}10^{-4}$ due to the smaller training set (${\\sim}$8K instances vs.\\ 70K for \\cls{}/\\cwm{} at function level); all other hyperparameters are shared.}\n131\t\\label{tab:hyperparams}\n132\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n133\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n134\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n135\t\\toprule\n136\t\\textbf{Hyperparameter} & \\textbf{Qwen3-4B \\cls{}} & \\textbf{Qwen3-4B \\cwm{}} & \\textbf{Qwen3-8B \\cls{}} & \\textbf{Qwen3-8B \\cwm{}} & \\textbf{DS-6.7B \\cls{}} & \\textbf{DS-6.7B \\cwm{}} \\\\\n137\t\\midrule\n138\tBase model & Qwen3-4B & Qwen3-4B & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B & DS-Coder-6.7B & DS-Coder-6.7B \\\\\n139\tLoRA rank ($r$) & 16 & 16 & 16 & 16 & 16 & 16 \\\\\n140\tLoRA alpha ($\\alpha$) & 32 & 32 & 32 & 32 & 32 & 32 \\\\\n141\tLoRA dropout & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 \\\\\n142\tLoRA targets & \\multicolumn{6}{c}{\\texttt{q\\_proj, k\\_proj, v\\_proj, o\\_proj}} \\\\\n143\tLearning rate & 5e-5 & 5e-5 & 5e-5 & 5e-5 & 2e-4 & 2e-4 \\\\\n144\tOptimizer & \\multicolumn{6}{c}{AdamW ($\\beta_1{=}0.9$, $\\beta_2{=}0.999$)} \\\\\n145\tWeight decay & 0.01 & 0.01 & 0.01 & 0.01 & 0.01 & 0.01 \\\\\n146\tWarmup ratio & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 & 0.05 \\\\\n147\tLR scheduler & \\multicolumn{6}{c}{Linear} \\\\\n148\tPer-device batch size & 2 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\\n149\tGradient accumulation & 16 & 32 & 32 & 32 & 16 & 32 \\\\\n150\tEffective batch size & 32 & 32 & 32 & 32 & 32 & 32 \\\\\n151\tMax sequence length & 2,048 & 2,048 & 2,048 & 2,048 & 2,048 & 2,048 \\\\\n152\tEpochs & 3 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 3 \\\\\n153\tPrecision & bf16 & bf16 & bf16 & bf16 & bf16 & bf16 \\\\\n154\tGradient checkpointing & \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark & \\checkmark \\\\\n155\tSeeds & 42, 123 & 42, 123 & 42, 123 & 42 & 42, 123 & 42, 123 \\\\\n156\tTraining samples & 70,748 & 70,748 & 70,748 & 70,748 & 70,748 & 70,748 \\\\\n157\t\\bottomrule\n158\t\\end{tabular}}\n159\t\\end{table}\n160\t\n161\t\\section{Val-Test Mutation Type Distribution}\n162\t\\label{app:mutation_distribution}\n163\t\n164\tTable~\\ref{tab:mutation_distribution} reports the mutation type composition of the repo-level validation and test sets. The most notable shift is \\texttt{operator\\_swap}, which nearly doubles from val (19.1\\%) to test (34.3\\%), while \\texttt{off\\_by\\_one} shrinks from 10.0\\% to 1.9\\%.\n165\t\n166\t\\begin{table}[h]\n167\t\\centering\n168\t\\small\n169\t\\caption{Repo-level mutation type distribution: validation vs.\\ test set, with per-type \\cls{} accuracy at two model sizes (seed 42). Shift = test\\% $-$ val\\%.}\n170\t\\label{tab:mutation_distribution}\n171\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n172\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n173\t\\toprule\n174\t\\textbf{Mutation Type} & \\textbf{Val (\\%)} & \\textbf{Test (\\%)} & \\textbf{Shift} & \\textbf{\\cls{} 4B} & \\textbf{\\cls{} 8B} \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tgold & 45.2 & 47.4 & +2.2 & 98.3 & 96.2 \\\\\n177\toperator\\_swap & 19.1 & 34.3 & +15.2 & 78.9 & 81.4 \\\\\n178\tboundary\\_removal & 15.0 & 10.7 & $-$4.3 & 46.9 & 46.9 \\\\\n179\treturn\\_value & 10.7 & 5.7 & $-$5.0 & 68.0 & 75.4 \\\\\n180\toff\\_by\\_one & 10.0 & 1.9 & $-$8.1 & 58.5 & 86.9 \\\\\n181\t\\bottomrule\n182\t\\end{tabular}\n183\t\\end{table}\n184\t\n185\tThe \\texttt{operator\\_swap} shift is the primary source of val-test distribution mismatch. Since \\cls{} achieves moderate accuracy on this type (78.9--81.4\\%), the over-representation in the test set does not systematically bias overall accuracy estimates in either direction. Two boundary observations are notable: (1)~\\texttt{boundary\\_removal} represents a scale-invariant difficulty ceiling at ${\\sim}$47\\% across both model sizes: this mutation type resists capacity scaling; (2)~\\texttt{off\\_by\\_one} shows the largest scaling gain (+28.4\\pp{}, 58.5\\%$\\to$86.9\\%) but its small test share (1.9\\%) limits its contribution to overall accuracy differences across scales.\n186\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"b4fb188e-53ec-425c-8958-88bbcbda2f45","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:55:20.867Z"}
