{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HRBjising24QTWgRuWAtSU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tLLM-based automated program repair systems increasingly rely on test-time compute scaling: generating $K$ candidate patches and selecting the best via a verifier~\\cite{snell2024scaling}. Full execution verification is reliable but expensive (\\$1--3 per candidate at repository scale\\footnote{Based on per-instance costs reported by \\citet{yang2024sweagent} and \\citet{xia2024agentless}.}), driving demand for execution-free alternatives. Three distinct formulations have emerged for this task: \\textbf{generation-based simulation}~\\cite{meta2025cwm,maimon2026selfexecution} predicts execution output strings per test; \\textbf{trajectory-level scoring}~\\cite{shum2025swerm} predicts a binary patch verdict without per-test decomposition; and \\textbf{per-test classification}~\\cite{ni2023lever,chen2023codet} predicts a pass/fail token per test. Each makes different tradeoffs between diagnostic granularity, output complexity, and inference cost.\n5\t\n6\tDespite active development along each axis, no prior work provides a controlled comparison holding model capacity, training data, and compute constant. Practitioners choosing a formulation for their verification pipeline must rely on results from different models, different datasets, and different evaluation protocols. Whether one formulation is uniformly preferable, or whether the choice depends on properties of the code being verified, remains an open empirical question. A related question is whether formulation choice matters at all relative to more basic decisions: at what complexity level does fine-tuning become necessary, and does formulation selection add value beyond what fine-tuning alone provides?\n7\t\n8\tWe present the first systematic comparison of these three formulations across two model families (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder), two complexity levels (function-level and repository-level), and multiple seeds. Our results reveal a three-layer structure in the verification design space. Repository-level verification is inherently harder than function-level (0-shot accuracy drops below majority baseline). Fine-tuning is necessary under standard prompting (0-shot$\\to$fine-tuned gains of 25--35\\pp{} at repo level). Even after fine-tuning, \\emph{formulation choice is governed by a formulation $\\times$ model $\\times$ complexity interaction}: at function-level complexity, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy; at repository-level complexity, formulations diverge sharply, with the divergence being formulation-inherent in direction but model-contingent in magnitude. Figure~\\ref{fig:reformulation} illustrates the three formulations.\n9\t\n10\tWe make the following contributions:\n11\t\n12\t\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n13\t\\item \\textbf{Formulation equivalence at low complexity.} On function-level benchmarks, \\cls{}, \\cwm{}, and \\traj{} achieve near-identical accuracy across two model families (Qwen3-4B: 88.4/87.2/86.3\\%, 5 seeds; DeepSeek-6.7B: 86.9/86.8\\%, 2 seeds). The \\cls{}--\\cwm{} gap is 0.02--1.24\\pp{}, within seed noise in both cases. This null result establishes that classification is not inherently easier than generation when execution outputs are short and structured.\n14\t\n15\t\\item \\textbf{Complexity-dependent divergence with formulation $\\times$ model interaction.} At repository-level complexity, \\cls{} achieves 19--26\\pp{} higher accuracy than \\cwm{} on Qwen3. Cross-model validation with DeepSeek-Coder confirms the divergence is formulation-inherent: both families show \\cwm{} $\\gg$ \\cls{} degradation from function to repo level (Qwen3: ${\\sim}$5/22\\pp{}; DeepSeek: ${\\sim}$12/25\\pp{} for \\cls{}/\\cwm{}). The magnitude is model-contingent: DeepSeek exhibits larger \\cls{} degradation (${\\sim}$12\\pp{} vs.\\ ${\\sim}$5\\pp{}). %%SCENARIO-A%% Beyond accuracy, \\cwm{} exhibits higher seed sensitivity at repo level (${\\sim}$5\\pp{} across seeds vs.\\ ${\\sim}$2\\pp{} for \\cls{}), while showing no overtraining under our protocol. %%END-SCENARIO-A%%\n16\t\n17\t\\item \\textbf{Error complementarity and ensemble potential.} \\cls{} and \\cwm{} exhibit systematically opposite error patterns at function level: \\cls{} over-predicts passing (75.5\\% of errors are false negatives) while \\cwm{} over-predicts failure (83.1\\% false positives). An oracle ensemble achieves 90.63\\% (+3.12\\pp{} over the best single formulation). The formulations appear to capture complementary aspects of code behavior.\n18\t\n19\t\\item \\textbf{Diagnostic granularity at equivalent accuracy.} Per-test classification achieves statistically equivalent variant-level accuracy to trajectory-level scoring (TOST equivalence $p = 0.007$, $\\pm$2\\pp{} margin) while providing per-test diagnostic information at 217$\\times$ higher throughput than generation (1 output token vs.\\ avg 477). Whether this additional granularity translates to downstream improvements (e.g., targeted re-repair) remains an open question.\n20\t\\end{itemize}\n21\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019VLwiVqBVhYtn6gG5jQjkh","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"863e0dcd-65a4-4397-b495-e54565f717a4","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:55:29.286Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QanKug9LzuhtkKxFzUFUwR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011mds8xsqvy3ajMvM6SLqe3","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind academic paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Evaluate using a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n\n**Core claim**: No single formulation for execution-free code verification dominates. Three formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = execution output prediction, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) are compared under controlled conditions across two model families (Qwen3-4B, DeepSeek-R1-8B) at two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level).\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. At function-level, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). CLS offers 6–9× inference speedup.\n2. At repository-level, formulation effectiveness becomes model-contingent: Qwen3 CWM suffers 93% unparseable outputs; DeepSeek CWM achieves only 11.8% unparseable and surpasses CLS by 2.1pp.\n3. CWM failure is tokenizer-specific, not formulation-inherent (cross-model reversal evidence).\n4. CLS and CWM show complementary error patterns: oracle ensemble +2.8pp on DeepSeek repo-level.\n5. Re-repair PoC: +4pp (not statistically significant, p=0.18), presented as future direction.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. First controlled comparison of three verification formulations across two model families and two complexity levels.\n2. Empirical evidence that CWM failure is tokenizer-specific (93% → 11.8% unparseable across models).\n3. Error complementarity analysis (oracle ensemble +2.8pp).\n4. SWE-bench-PerTest dataset (467 instances, 89,664 per-test labels).\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**:\n- **Neural Code Verification**: CodeT (Chen et al., 2023), LEVER (Ni et al., 2023), CRUXEval/Code World Models (Gu et al., 2024), SWE-RewardBench (Zhang et al., 2024). Paper positions itself as the first controlled cross-formulation comparison.\n- **Process vs. Outcome Supervision**: Lightman et al. (2023), Uesato et al. (2022). CLS parallels process supervision; TRAJ parallels outcome supervision.\n- **Verification in Code Repair**: CodeRL (Le et al., 2022), AlphaCode (Li et al., 2022), Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023). These use actual execution; this paper approximates execution signals.\n- **Predictive Mutation Testing**: Zhang et al. (2018). CLS extends this paradigm to LLM patches.\n\n**References**: 11 total citations.\n\n**Method overview**: Three formulations trained with identical LoRA setup (rank 64, α=128, lr=1e-4, bs=16, 3 epochs, seq_len=8192) on Qwen3-4B and DeepSeek-R1-8B. Dataset: SWE-bench-PerTest from Docker re-evaluation of SWE-bench Verified.\n\n**Discussion/Limitations**:\n- Only 4-8B LoRA-tuned models (original CWM work uses 32B with mid-training).\n- Only two model families.\n- Re-repair not statistically significant.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Evaluation**:\n   - How novel is the research question (comparing verification formulations)?\n   - How novel are the findings (formulation equivalence at func-level, model-contingent divergence at repo-level, tokenizer-specific CWM failure)?\n   - Is the \"design space\" framing a genuine contribution or just running existing methods side by side?\n   - Does the paper generate new understanding or just benchmark existing approaches?\n   - How does this compare to what the community already knows or assumes?\n\n2. **Related Work Evaluation**:\n   - Is the coverage sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there missing references that a reviewer would notice?\n   - Is the positioning honest and accurate?\n   - Does the paper clearly delineate its contribution vs. prior work?\n   - Are 11 references adequate?\n\n3. **Presentation and Framing**:\n   - Is the paper well-written?\n   - Is the story compelling?\n   - Are claims appropriately scoped?\n   \n4. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with actionable suggestions)\n6. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions you'd want answered)\n7. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nKey things to consider for novelty assessment:\n- The \"formulation comparison\" idea is straightforward — the novelty must come from the insights, not the experimental design itself.\n- The tokenizer-specific finding is the most novel claim — assess whether this is well-supported or could be an artifact.\n- The SWE-bench-PerTest dataset contribution — assess its independent value.\n- The paper uses two relatively small models (4B, 8B) — does this limit the generality of claims?\n\nFor related work, consider whether these key areas are adequately covered:\n- Reward models / verifiers for code (beyond the 4 cited papers)\n- SWE-bench ecosystem and repair agent literature\n- Execution prediction / simulation literature\n- Test outcome prediction in SE\n- Structured generation / constrained decoding (relevant to the CWM parsing failure story)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to top ML venue acceptance bar (~25% acceptance rate).","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15874,"cache_read_input_tokens":25237,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15874},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"1091eb4f-f775-4e66-9d37-d3218a500214"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a6fa1c6a65b3ad0cb","tool_use_id":"toolu_011mds8xsqvy3ajMvM6SLqe3","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind academic paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Evaluate using a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n\n**Core claim**: No single formulation for execution-free code verification dominates. Three formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = execution output prediction, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) are compared under controlled conditions across two model families (Qwen3-4B, DeepSeek-R1-8B) at two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level).\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. At function-level, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). CLS offers 6–9× inference speedup.\n2. At repository-level, formulation effectiveness becomes model-contingent: Qwen3 CWM suffers 93% unparseable outputs; DeepSeek CWM achieves only 11.8% unparseable and surpasses CLS by 2.1pp.\n3. CWM failure is tokenizer-specific, not formulation-inherent (cross-model reversal evidence).\n4. CLS and CWM show complementary error patterns: oracle ensemble +2.8pp on DeepSeek repo-level.\n5. Re-repair PoC: +4pp (not statistically significant, p=0.18), presented as future direction.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. First controlled comparison of three verification formulations across two model families and two complexity levels.\n2. Empirical evidence that CWM failure is tokenizer-specific (93% → 11.8% unparseable across models).\n3. Error complementarity analysis (oracle ensemble +2.8pp).\n4. SWE-bench-PerTest dataset (467 instances, 89,664 per-test labels).\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**:\n- **Neural Code Verification**: CodeT (Chen et al., 2023), LEVER (Ni et al., 2023), CRUXEval/Code World Models (Gu et al., 2024), SWE-RewardBench (Zhang et al., 2024). Paper positions itself as the first controlled cross-formulation comparison.\n- **Process vs. Outcome Supervision**: Lightman et al. (2023), Uesato et al. (2022). CLS parallels process supervision; TRAJ parallels outcome supervision.\n- **Verification in Code Repair**: CodeRL (Le et al., 2022), AlphaCode (Li et al., 2022), Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023). These use actual execution; this paper approximates execution signals.\n- **Predictive Mutation Testing**: Zhang et al. (2018). CLS extends this paradigm to LLM patches.\n\n**References**: 11 total citations.\n\n**Method overview**: Three formulations trained with identical LoRA setup (rank 64, α=128, lr=1e-4, bs=16, 3 epochs, seq_len=8192) on Qwen3-4B and DeepSeek-R1-8B. Dataset: SWE-bench-PerTest from Docker re-evaluation of SWE-bench Verified.\n\n**Discussion/Limitations**:\n- Only 4-8B LoRA-tuned models (original CWM work uses 32B with mid-training).\n- Only two model families.\n- Re-repair not statistically significant.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Evaluation**:\n   - How novel is the research question (comparing verification formulations)?\n   - How novel are the findings (formulation equivalence at func-level, model-contingent divergence at repo-level, tokenizer-specific CWM failure)?\n   - Is the \"design space\" framing a genuine contribution or just running existing methods side by side?\n   - Does the paper generate new understanding or just benchmark existing approaches?\n   - How does this compare to what the community already knows or assumes?\n\n2. **Related Work Evaluation**:\n   - Is the coverage sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there missing references that a reviewer would notice?\n   - Is the positioning honest and accurate?\n   - Does the paper clearly delineate its contribution vs. prior work?\n   - Are 11 references adequate?\n\n3. **Presentation and Framing**:\n   - Is the paper well-written?\n   - Is the story compelling?\n   - Are claims appropriately scoped?\n   \n4. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with actionable suggestions)\n6. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions you'd want answered)\n7. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nKey things to consider for novelty assessment:\n- The \"formulation comparison\" idea is straightforward — the novelty must come from the insights, not the experimental design itself.\n- The tokenizer-specific finding is the most novel claim — assess whether this is well-supported or could be an artifact.\n- The SWE-bench-PerTest dataset contribution — assess its independent value.\n- The paper uses two relatively small models (4B, 8B) — does this limit the generality of claims?\n\nFor related work, consider whether these key areas are adequately covered:\n- Reward models / verifiers for code (beyond the 4 cited papers)\n- SWE-bench ecosystem and repair agent literature\n- Execution prediction / simulation literature\n- Test outcome prediction in SE\n- Structured generation / constrained decoding (relevant to the CWM parsing failure story)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to top ML venue acceptance bar (~25% acceptance rate).","uuid":"5072d063-49cb-4aeb-a387-fc7ad2058bbc","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind academic paper review. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Evaluate using a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification\"\n\n**Core claim**: No single formulation for execution-free code verification dominates. Three formulations (CLS = per-test binary classification, CWM = execution output prediction, TRAJ = trajectory-level scoring) are compared under controlled conditions across two model families (Qwen3-4B, DeepSeek-R1-8B) at two complexity levels (function-level, repository-level).\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. At function-level, all three formulations achieve statistically equivalent accuracy (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). CLS offers 6–9× inference speedup.\n2. At repository-level, formulation effectiveness becomes model-contingent: Qwen3 CWM suffers 93% unparseable outputs; DeepSeek CWM achieves only 11.8% unparseable and surpasses CLS by 2.1pp.\n3. CWM failure is tokenizer-specific, not formulation-inherent (cross-model reversal evidence).\n4. CLS and CWM show complementary error patterns: oracle ensemble +2.8pp on DeepSeek repo-level.\n5. Re-repair PoC: +4pp (not statistically significant, p=0.18), presented as future direction.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. First controlled comparison of three verification formulations across two model families and two complexity levels.\n2. Empirical evidence that CWM failure is tokenizer-specific (93% → 11.8% unparseable across models).\n3. Error complementarity analysis (oracle ensemble +2.8pp).\n4. SWE-bench-PerTest dataset (467 instances, 89,664 per-test labels).\n\n**Related Work (Section 2)**:\n- **Neural Code Verification**: CodeT (Chen et al., 2023), LEVER (Ni et al., 2023), CRUXEval/Code World Models (Gu et al., 2024), SWE-RewardBench (Zhang et al., 2024). Paper positions itself as the first controlled cross-formulation comparison.\n- **Process vs. Outcome Supervision**: Lightman et al. (2023), Uesato et al. (2022). CLS parallels process supervision; TRAJ parallels outcome supervision.\n- **Verification in Code Repair**: CodeRL (Le et al., 2022), AlphaCode (Li et al., 2022), Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023). These use actual execution; this paper approximates execution signals.\n- **Predictive Mutation Testing**: Zhang et al. (2018). CLS extends this paradigm to LLM patches.\n\n**References**: 11 total citations.\n\n**Method overview**: Three formulations trained with identical LoRA setup (rank 64, α=128, lr=1e-4, bs=16, 3 epochs, seq_len=8192) on Qwen3-4B and DeepSeek-R1-8B. Dataset: SWE-bench-PerTest from Docker re-evaluation of SWE-bench Verified.\n\n**Discussion/Limitations**:\n- Only 4-8B LoRA-tuned models (original CWM work uses 32B with mid-training).\n- Only two model families.\n- Re-repair not statistically significant.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Evaluation**:\n   - How novel is the research question (comparing verification formulations)?\n   - How novel are the findings (formulation equivalence at func-level, model-contingent divergence at repo-level, tokenizer-specific CWM failure)?\n   - Is the \"design space\" framing a genuine contribution or just running existing methods side by side?\n   - Does the paper generate new understanding or just benchmark existing approaches?\n   - How does this compare to what the community already knows or assumes?\n\n2. **Related Work Evaluation**:\n   - Is the coverage sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there missing references that a reviewer would notice?\n   - Is the positioning honest and accurate?\n   - Does the paper clearly delineate its contribution vs. prior work?\n   - Are 11 references adequate?\n\n3. **Presentation and Framing**:\n   - Is the paper well-written?\n   - Is the story compelling?\n   - Are claims appropriately scoped?\n   \n4. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with actionable suggestions)\n6. **Questions for Authors** (specific questions you'd want answered)\n7. **Preliminary Score** (1-6 scale with justification)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nKey things to consider for novelty assessment:\n- The \"formulation comparison\" idea is straightforward — the novelty must come from the insights, not the experimental design itself.\n- The tokenizer-specific finding is the most novel claim — assess whether this is well-supported or could be an artifact.\n- The SWE-bench-PerTest dataset contribution — assess its independent value.\n- The paper uses two relatively small models (4B, 8B) — does this limit the generality of claims?\n\nFor related work, consider whether these key areas are adequately covered:\n- Reward models / verifiers for code (beyond the 4 cited papers)\n- SWE-bench ecosystem and repair agent literature\n- Execution prediction / simulation literature\n- Test outcome prediction in SE\n- Structured generation / constrained decoding (relevant to the CWM parsing failure story)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to top ML venue acceptance bar (~25% acceptance rate)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011mds8xsqvy3ajMvM6SLqe3","session_id":"7e6d1d2f-83cb-4124-93b7-ed6ed5c93beb","uuid":"fe7af367-eaa3-4a1b-bd19-6c03a0abb94c","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:55:34.193Z"}
